concepts.content:
The difficulty of optimizing VAEs lies in the fact that the objective is intractable, requiring integration over an infinite set of deep net forward passes. Once an approximation is derived, optimization can be performed via backpropagation. With the objective and hypothesis given, the VAE learning problem is defined as:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \theta^* &= \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_{\theta} L(\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, \theta)\\
    &= \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_{\theta} \sum_{i=1}^N \log \underbrace{\int_{\mathbf{z}} \overbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}; g^{\mu}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}), g^{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}))}^{p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)} \| \mathbf{z})} \overbrace{\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{z}; \mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})}^{p_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{z})}d\mathbf{z}}_{p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)})}
\end{aligned}$$

